<!--
    An inline system call (`svc`) lifts to a CALLOTHER with no register inputs
    and no output, so the x0 a function sets up for it still holds that value
    afterwards. `pair` returns the two argument registers it set up as a register pair, and
    `after` doubles its argument: it reads back the x0 it saved, not the result.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 flow-classification.
    P0 assertion:      option syscallregs off|auto|on  (default auto).

    Source (clang -O2 -target aarch64-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes verbatim below):

        long pair(long a, long b) {
          register long x0 asm("x0") = b; register long x1 asm("x1") = a;
          register long x8 asm("x8") = 64;
          asm volatile("svc 0" : "+r"(x0) : "r"(x1), "r"(x8) : "memory");
          return x0;
        }
        long after(long x) {
          register long x0 asm("x0"); register long x8 asm("x8") = 172;
          asm volatile("svc 0" : "=r"(x0) : "r"(x8) : "memory");
          if (x0 < 0) return -1;
          return x0 + x;
        }
        long fence(long a) { asm volatile("dmb ish" ::: "memory"); return a; }

    Pass 1 (option syscallregs on) gives the svc its effects: it writes x0,
    and it reads the number register x8 and then the argument registers in
    order, up to the first one the function did not write on every path to
    it.  Pass 2 (option syscallregs off) is the old model, and so is pass 3,
    the default `auto`: it acts only on an image the loader identified as a
    program for an operating system's user space, which a bare byte chunk is
    not.  fence is the control: its barrier is a CALLOTHER too,
    and nothing about it changes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
